<p>Let's look at what gets created once the harness is composed. Claude Code creates the agents, skills, and so on <strong>under the <code>.claude</code> folder</strong>.</p>

<h3>The agents that were created (example)</h3>
<p>For the "national R&D budget · post-PBS" task, this was the team that got composed.</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Quantitative R&D budget analyst</strong> &mdash; breaks the budget allocation · adjustment plan down by ministry · program · year, and tracks changes in the government/government-funded ratio.</li>
    <li><strong>Governance · finance expert for government-funded institutions</strong> &mdash; derives the strategy for the transition from PBS to a mission-oriented system.</li>
    <li><strong>National R&D policy researcher</strong> &mdash; researches budget · policy documents · press releases · NIS/NSTC agenda items and the like from primary sources.</li>
    <li><strong>Report writing agent</strong> &mdash; gathers the preceding outputs and writes them up as a report.</li>
    <li><strong>QA reviewer</strong> &mdash; cross-checks not simple typos but <strong>claim–source consistency, numerical consistency, and the logical connection of the recommendations</strong>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>The skills that get injected</h3>
<p>Agents alone are not enough, so specialized <strong>skills</strong> are created and injected. For example: a quantitative budget analysis method (standard forms · calculation rules · checklist), citation verification for policy reports (verification axes · priorities), and a government policy report writing format (table of contents).</p>

<h3>Orchestrator (always one)</h3>
<p>It is the <strong>PM role</strong> that coordinates the agents so they do not wander off. It does not look into the detailed work; it only watches "are the agents doing their jobs well". From Phase 0 on, <strong>the workflow is planned in advance</strong> as to which agent each stage needs. (It feels similar to Claude Code's plan mode.)</p>

<h3>Review and getting ready to run</h3>
<ul>
    <li>The review step is mandatory. If you give <strong>feedback</strong> like "add more agents", "this does not match the intent, fix it", or "revise the harness", agents/skills are newly created or reinforced.</li>
    <li>There is an issue where agents <strong>are not loaded dynamically</strong>. If you do not see them in <code>/agents</code>, they load once you <code>/exit</code> and come back in.</li>
</ul>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">The _workspace folder</span>
    When you run with the harness, a <code>_workspace</code> folder appears where the <strong>intermediate outputs</strong> the agents produced collect &mdash; the first-pass research, the budget analysis, the QA report (critical · major · minor issues) and its fixes, the limitations, and even the <strong>supporting sources</strong>. Because it is source-based, hallucination is greatly reduced.
</div>
